      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! In the realm of residential deck construction, the live load is the weight of the deck itself, including the furniture, people, and any other temporary objects that might be placed on it. It's like the weight of a bunch of squatting, grumbling goblins, each one adding a bit more pressure on the wooden planks. On the other hand, the dead load is the weight of the deck's own structure, the beams, joists, and the cedar boards themselves. It's like the weight of a solid, immovable goblin army, always there, no matter what.\n\nNow, when it comes to snow load, think of it as the weight of a heavy snowfall, piling up on your deck like a mountain of snow. In British Columbia, the building standards will give you a specific value for the snow load based on the location and elevation of your deck. This value is like a magical goblin spell that determines how much snow your deck can handle before it starts to crumble under the pressure. So, you'll need to consult the BC building standards to find out the exact snow load value for your area and make sure your deck can handle it. Just like a wise old goblin who knows the secrets of the land, you'll",
